# A vault's .gitignore, seeded by Lipika (github.com/dnsco/lipika).
# Every entry below is here because tracking it caused a problem, not for tidiness.

.DS_Store

# Obsidian writes per-machine UI state alongside config you DO want tracked:
# app.json, appearance.json, core-plugins.json and snippets/ are settings and belong in git.
# These four are window geometry and a derived index, and they churn on every launch.
.obsidian/graph.json
.obsidian/workspace.json
.obsidian/workspace-mobile.json
.obsidian/cache

# The shared pass log — every role's start/stop record, appended concurrently from every
# worktree. Tracking it turns each pair of parallel passes into a merge conflict.
# Consequence to expect: `lipika pass-invariants` reports its anchors check red, because the
# sha it wants to diff from lives in a file git does not see. That is correct, not a defect.
pass-log.jsonl

# Agent worktrees are provisioned inside the vault, so a tracked one nests the repo in itself
# — and a recursive grep from the root then double-counts every hit. Here rather than in
# .git/info/exclude, because exclude does not survive a clone.
.claude/worktrees/
.claude/settings.local.json

# Agent-to-agent reports: a scope's manifest, a brief written once and spawned
# against. Machinery state, not corpus — nothing in the vault cites it and no document's meaning
# depends on it, which is the same argument that keeps pass-log.jsonl untracked. Filenames carry
# the HEAD sha they were taken at, so a stale report is detectable rather than quietly trusted.
.lipika/

# Bytecode, if the vault carries analysis scripts of its own.
__pycache__/
*.pyc
